            <ArticleTitle>Management of Sleep Quality and Pain in an Individual Living with HIV and Hepatitis C Coinfection Using an Activity Monitor.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>476-81</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1177/2325957415601502</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Case report.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Twenty-five percent of people living with HIV/AIDS (PLWHA) are also coinfected with hepatitis C virus (HCV). There are limited reports to assist in the symptom management of PLWHA and HCV coinfection.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE DESCRIPTION" NlmCategory="METHODS">Our case was a 67-year-old man living with HIV/HCV coinfection and other medical complications. Our intervention consisted of a 6-week progressive conditioning program, while his activity and sleep pattern were monitored using a wrist accelerometer.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OUTCOMES" NlmCategory="RESULTS">After completing a conditioning training, there was a 64% decrease in the number of waking episodes per night and 68% decrease in activity at night. Based on Pittsburgh Sleep Quality Index scores, the participant transitioned from being a &quot;poor sleeper&quot; to a &quot;good sleeper.&quot; A 40% decrease in pain was reported. Functional outcome measures also showed improvements.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DISCUSSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">There were improvements in the participant's sleep quality and pain. The use of a commercial accelerometer may assist in objectively tracking compliance and activity changes.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© The Author(s) 2015.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
